*	15,000 MT of Triple Super Phosphate at US$ 262.74 per MT to Valency International Trading Pte. Ltd.;

*	13,600 MT of Muriate of Potash at US$ 317.25 per MT to Agri Commodities and Finance; and

*	3,060 MT Urea (Prilled) for Ceylon Fertilizer Company Limited  at US$ 285.00 per MT to Agri Commodities and Finance.

- The eradication of poverty in the country has been identified as one of the top priorities of the Government in order to create an inclusive and suitable development under the National Policy Framework of the Government - “Saubagyaye Dakma”. Further, the Development Goals identified in the said National Policy Framework of the Government are consistent with the 17 Sustainable Development Goals of the United Nations. Accordingly, the proposal made by H.E. the President to implement the following proposals on priority basis with a view to eradicating the poverty in the country, was approved by the Cabinet.

*	Implementing a programme ensuring the food security of low income families by providing them with essential food items;

*	The programme for providing 100,000 jobs for 100,000 poor families;

*	Implementing the programme for providing the poor families with houses under the programme of “One House - One Village” on an accelerated basis;

*	Implementing the rural road development programme of 100,000 km to provide quality road access facilities for at least 1,000,000 households; and

*	Providing easy access to credit prioritizing the small business development, self-employment and household needs and household level livelihood development  initiatives.

- The Cabinet at its meeting held on 2019 10 29 had granted approval to sign a compact Agreement and a Programme Implementation Agreement between the Millennium Challenge Corporation of the USA and the Government of Sri Lanka to obtain the grant funds provided by the Office of the Millennium Challenge Corporation for the implementation of several programmes of the identified fields. However, taking into consideration the views expressed on the project by various parties, the proposal made by the Hon. Prime Minister to appoint a Committee comprising of the following to make a comprehensive study on the Millennium Challenge Corporation Project and submit recommendations to the Government and to suspend taking action in terms of  the Cabinet decision dated 2019 10 29 in this respect, until such time that the Committee makes its recommendations, was approved by the Cabinet.

*	Prof. Lalithasiri Gunaruwan - (Chairman);

*	Mr. D.S. Jayaweera,
	former Secretary to the Ministry of Transport;

*	Mr. Nihal Jayawardena P.C.; and

*	Mr. Nalaka Jayaweera.

- The proposal made by H.E. the President to appoint the Cabinet Sub Committee on Cost of Living under the Chairmanship of H.E. the President comprising the following members to make recommendations to the Cabinet regarding the matters concerning the cost of living, was approved by the Cabinet.

*	Hon. the Prime Minister in his capacity as the Minister of Finance, Economy and Policy Development;

*	The Minister of Mahaweli, Agriculture, Irrigation and Rural Development and the Minister of Internal Trade, Food Security and Consumer Welfare;

*	The Minister of Fisheries & Aquatic Resources; and

*	The Minister of Plantation Industries and Export Agriculture

It was also decided to appoint a Committee comprising of 06 State Ministers and 12 Officials to assist the said Sub Committee.

In providing an efficient passenger transportation service to the general public by improving of the Public Transport Service, the Government has identified the development of the Railway Service of the country as a priority. Accordingly, action has been taken to commence the development activities of the suburban railway system connecting the Colombo city with other areas under the 'Colombo Suburban Railway Project' implemented under the financial assistance of the Asian Development Bank (ADB). A proposal has been made to implement the following few small scale projects required for the improvement of the railway efficiency through 'Colombo Suburban Railway Efficiency Improvement Project (CSREIP)' to be implemented under this project:

*	Implementation of a modern Ticketing and Seating Reservation System,  replacing the existing ticketing and seating reservation system;

*	Installation of a new system for providing radio telecommunication services to the railway passengers;
*	Expansion and reconstruction of the School Avenue railway bridge at Maligawatta, Colombo;

*	Construction of the Colombo Train Control Centre and the Railway  Operations Centre;

*	Development of the training activities of the Sri Lanka - German Railway Technical Training Centre, including the new technology, systems and methodologies;

*	Providing a Machinery and Carriage Repair workshop for the Railway Mechanical Engineers' Department at Ratmalana;

*	Establishment of 02 Housing Schemes for the resettlement of families living in the railway reservations.

As such, the proposal made by Hon. Nimal Siripala de Silva, the Minister of Transport and Civil Aviation, to implement the Colombo Suburban Railway Efficiency Improvement Project (CSREIP) which includes the above sub projects, was approved by the Cabinet.

- Even though there exists around 970 primary level hospitals in Sri Lanka, there is a trend among the general public to bypass this network of hospitals and to move towards the hospitals providing treatments at tertiary level. Owing to this trend, a heavy congestion has been created in such hospitals providing treatments at tertiary level. Besides, the need has arisen to enhance the local Healthcare service due the increase of the elderly population, growth of non‑communicable diseases, dynamic illness patterns etc. As such, a proposal has been made to establish an advanced Health Service System, to enable the general public to have access to those facilities easily by linking the closest hospitals providing specialized treatment services, as forums.  

	Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. (Dr.) Rajitha Senaratne, the Minister of Health, Nutrition and Indigenous Medicine, to implement the 'Policy providing Healthcare services with the objective of Universal Health Coverage', prepared inclusive of the following components, was approved by the Cabinet.

*	to identify one family doctor each to cover per 5,000 of the population and to further strengthen the institutions providing Primary Healthcare services to facilitate the prevention of illnesses;

*	to provide the Medical Practitioners with the initial training required for carrying out the functions in the field of Family Medicine and for  strengthening the first contact care services;

*	to ensure that all patients are provided with treatments at the Primary Healthcare Institutions and to encourage to refer those patients to secondary and tertiary hospitals with Specialist Doctors appropriately, in  a timely manner;

*	to provide a Personal Health Record to every citizen, with a unique heath identification number and to further strengthen the Health Information Management System;

*	to ensure the attribution and the utilization of human resources in the health sector productively;

*	to establish a method of obtaining distant assistance from Specialist Doctors to help the doctors providing Primary Healthcare within the relevant forums, in order to enhance their clinical skills.

The population in the Kandy district is around 1.4 million and out of which about 150,000 are the residents of the Kandy Metropolitan Region. Daily inflow of vehicles to the KMR is about 55,000 and the 3 access roads available are not at all sufficient for the fulfillment of the present and the future  transport requirements and the roads cannot be further widened due to extensive urbanization. Due to this reason, a proposal has been made to implement a Kandy Suburban Railway Development Project to minimize the acute traffic congestion prevalent in Kandy and the Asian Development Bank has expressed its willingness to provide loan assistance for the purpose.  Accordingly, the information submitted to the Cabinet by Hon. Nimal Siripala de Silva, the Minister of Transport and Civil Aviation, to the effect that proposals have been called from appropriate institutions to obtain consultancy services necessary for the followings road development activities, was noted by the Cabinet.    

*	Increasing the railway traffic capacity of the main line from Rambukkana to Kadugannawa;

*	Construction of Double Lines from Kadugannawa to Katugastota or Wallegagama (Matale Line);

*	Rehabilitation of the track section from Katugastota to Matale;

*	Improvement of the traffic capacity in the section from Peradeniya junction to Gampola.

The proposal made by Hon. Mangala Samaraweera, the Minister of Finance and Mass Media, to amend the Nation Building Tax (NBT) (Amendment) Bill incorporating the following amendments proposed by the Budget 2018, was approved by the Cabinet:

*	Imposing the NBT on liquor in addition to the other existing taxes applicable to liquor;

*	Removal of the NBT from the following services and articles;

(a)	Equipment that enables advanced technology agriculture  practices, including green houses, poly tunnels and material for the construction of green houses;

(b)	Coconut oil and other coconut related products;

(c)	Gem Stones imported for the purpose of re export after cutting and polishing;

(d)	Non motorized equipment and accessories for water sports such as Rowing, Kayaks, Canoes, Kite Surfing  and Diving;

(e)	Non powered equipment and accessories for aero sports such as Hang Gliding, Ballooning, Dirigibles, Parachutes and Para Gliders;

(f)	Services provided by the Sri Lanka Deposit Insurance Scheme;

- The Government of Sri Lanka, in collaboration with the United Nations World Food Programme (WFP), has successfully completed a large number of various programmes since the year 1968. The following objectives are expected to be achieved by the implementation of the the strategic programmes planned by the Government of Sri Lanka in collaboration with the World Food Programme (WFP), during the period from 2018 - 2022  in accordance with  the United Nations Sustainable Development Framework. 

*	Supply of food all year round for the people affected by crisis situations;

*	Supply of food all year round for the children of school going age in the food insecure areas;

*	Improvement of the nutrition of children under 5 years, adolescent girls and women of the reproductive age, by the year 2025;

*	Strengthen the livelihood of easily vulnerable communities and the smallholder farmers to develop the ability of facing the risk situations;

The World Food Programme  has expressed its willingness to provide US$ 46.8 million for the implementation of the said strategic plan. Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Ranil Wickremasinghe, the Prime Minister in his capacity as the Minister of National Policies and Economic Affairs, to enter into a Memorandum of Understanding with the World Food Programme to obtain the grant aid and to get the strategic plan executed through the relevant line Ministries, was approved by the Cabinet.

- The proposal made by Hon. Lakshman Kiriella, the Minister of Higher Education and Highways, to award the following contracts for the development  of the infrastructure facilities in the University of Colombo, the Buddhist and Pali University of Sri Lanka and the Trincomalee Campus of the Eastern University, as recommended by the Standing Cabinet Appointed Procurement Committee (SCAPC), was approved by the Cabinet: 

(i)	awarding of the contract for the construction of a Student Service Centre for the Faculty of Science of the University of Colombo, for a sum of  Rs.381.49 million, to the State Engineering Corporation of Sri Lanka;

(ii)	awarding of the contract for the construction of a language skills development centre and an examination hall in the Buddhist and Pali University of Sri Lanka, for a sum of Rs.215.42 million, to M/s Central Engineering Services (Pvt.) Ltd.;
(iii)	awarding of the contract for the construction of a multi purpose auditorium for the Trincomalee Campus of the Eastern University, for a sum of Rs.674.47 million, to M/s Link Engineering (Pvt.) Ltd.;  

(iv)	awarding of the contract for the construction of a building complex for the Faculty of Communication and Business Studies of the Eastern University, for a sum of Rs.701.18 million, to M/s Edward and Christie (Pvt.) Ltd.

-	The functions and the administrative setup of the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration - SLIDA, the pioneer public sector training institute of Sri Lanka has been subject to changes during the last few years. Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Ranjith Madduma Bandara, the Minister of Public Administration and Management, to instruct the Legal Draftsman to amend the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration  Act, No.9 of 1982 concurrently, with the inclusion of the following amendments,  to effect the changes, was approved by the Cabinet.   


*	amendments to the organizational structure, objectives and functions and authorities, of the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration to suit the the present status and the requirements; 

*	inclusion of new provisions in the Act to reflect the functions and the authorities of the School of Postgraduate Studies of the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration; 

*	In accordance with the Cabinet approval already granted, the inclusion of new provisions in the Act to reflect the functions and the authorities of the Examination Unit of the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration.

- It has been proposed to streamline and update the existing Visa system to coincide with the Government economic policy to enhance the local and international investments for the development process of Sri Lanka. Presently, Visit Visa, Residence Visa, and Transit Visa categories are issued on the request of the qualified foreign nationals. The Cabinet Committee on Economic Management (CCEM) has recommended to amend the existing Visa categories and the charges made on them and to impose the Visa charges made at present in Sri Lankan Rupees, in American Dollars.  Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. S.B. Navinne, the Minister of Internal Affairs, Wayamba Development and Cultural Affairs, to include the following items also into method of visa issuance and to amend section 14  of the Immigrants and Emigrants Act, No.20 of 1948 and the relevant regulations as appropriate, was approved by the Cabinet.  

*	to vest the authority with the Secretary to the Ministry in charge of the subject of BOI to recommend Residence Visas for the investors/employees under the Board of Investments (BOI) sector;

*	to charge an overstay penalty of US$ 500 in addition to the applicable visa fee from foreigners who have overstayed the valid visa period;

*	to issue Residence Visas for students to the entire period of their study;

*	to issue a ten year Residence Visa for foreign nationals who make a remittance of over US$ 500,000 in to Sri Lanka;

*	to issue a five year Spouse Visa for the foreign spouse married to a Sri Lankan citizen and also their children;



*	to issue a Residence Visa for employment of foreign spouses of those demised Sri Lanka spouses who are living in Sri Lanka continuously for 10 years or who have children below the age of 18 years for employment for a period of 02 years; and

*	to issue a Permanent Residence Visa for the ex-Sri Lankan who is unable to apply for dual citizenship and already have a citizenship of a foreign country.

- The Cabinet discussed at length about the difficulties faced by the general public owing to the strike initiated by several categories of employees of the Department of Sri Lanka Railways. Accordingly, with a view to finding a satisfactory solution to meet the demands put forward by the relevant trade unions, the Cabinet decided to appoint the following Ministerial Committee under the Chairmanship of Hon.(Dr.) Sarath Amunugama, the Minister of Special Assignments, to conduct discussions with the representatives of the relevant trade unions and make recommendations without any delay for resolving the salaries and other professional issues of the railway employees and  an Officials' Committee comprising of the relevant officers to assist in its deliberations. 

*	Hon. (Dr.) Sarath Amunugama,
	Minister of Special Assignments - (Chairman);

*	Hon. (Dr.) Rajitha Senaratne,
	Minister of Health, Nutrition and Indigenous Medicine;

*	Hon. Ranjith Maddumabandara,
	Minister of Public Administration and Management;

*	Hon. Akila Viraj Kariyawasam, 
	Minister of Education.

- The disruption of cultivation in several seasons, due to the adverse climatic condition that prevailed has resulted in a considerable decline of food production in the country and has caused the escalation of prices of food items. Hon. Ranil Wickremasinghe, the Prime Minister pointed out to the Cabinet that the general public are being severely inconvenienced due to the exorbitant prices of the food items especially such as rice, coconut and vegetables varieties and the Cabinet discussed the matter at length.   

	With a view to controlling this situation, it was decided to take the following measures expeditiously to supply adequate essential food items to the market:

*	to facilitate any Private Institution or the Co-operative Wholesale Establishment (CWE) to import rice under the direction and supervision of the General Treasury;

*	to import the coconut kernel required for the coconut related industries and coconuts required for domestic consumption, under the supervision of the Ministry of Plantation Industries;

*	to appoint an Officials' Committee to submit proposals expeditiously on the further reliefs that could be provided, in addition to the reliefs already granted by the Government,  to the  farmers whose cultivation has been disrupted.

- The Sri Lanka Ports Authority (SLPA), Government of the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka (GOSL), China Merchants Port Holdings Company Limited (CM Port), Hambantota International Port Group (Private) Limited (HIPG) and the Hambantota International Port Services Company (Private) Limited (HIPS) entered into a Concession Agreement on 2017-07-29 pertaining to the development of the Hambantota Port by a Public-Private Partnership (PPP).  As per the provisions of the aforesaid Concession Agreement, the relevant parties should fulfill the condition precedents in the Agreement within 180 days from the date of entering into the Agreement and the activities pertaining to same are presently being carried out.   

In terms of clause 4.2 of the Agreement, after completing the condition precedents the CM Port or the affiliated institutions should make the payment, the 'Investment Value' to the Government of Sri Lanka as follows, - 

(i)	an amount of US$ 5 million has already been deposited in a special account as security deposit and the said amount should be transferred to the Sri Lanka Ports Authority on the effective date of the Agreement; 

(ii)	a 10% of the balance Investment Value (tranche 01) should be paid, after deducting the Security Deposit  within  the period of one month from the effective date of the Agreement; 
(iii)	a 30% of the  Investment Value (tranche 02) should be paid within the period of three months from the effective date of the Agreement; 

(iv)	a 60% of the  Investment Value (tranche 03) should be paid within the period of six months from the effective date of the Agreement. 

As requested by the Government of Sri Lanka the CM Port has agreed to advance the date of payment of 'Investment Value' and to increase the amount payable on the effective date of the Agreement as follows - 

(i)	payment of 30% of the remaining balance Investment Value (tranche 01) after deducting the Security Deposit  on the effective date of the Agreement; 

(ii)	payment of 10% (tranche 02) of the Investment Value within the period of one month from the effective date of Agreement; 

(iii)	payment of the balance 60% (tranche 03) of the Investment Value within the period of six months from the effective date of Agreement. 

As such, the proposal made by Hon. Mahinda Samarasinghe, the Minister of Ports and Shipping, to amend the relevant clauses of the Concession Agreement to enable to make the relevant payments as stated above and to sign an Addendum to the Agreement, was approved by the Cabinet.

- Considering the necessity of developing the accommodation facilities in the State Universities available presently, the Government has accorded high priority to develop the infrastructure facilities needed in the Universities.  Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Lakshman Kiriella, the Minister of Higher Education and Highways, to implement the following projects at an estimated cost of Rs.380 million between the period from 2018 - 2019, was approved by the Cabinet. 

*	Upgrading the Electrical and Electronic Engineering Department of the Faculty of Engineering of the University of Peradeniya;

*	Construction of a 03 storey hostel building for the foreign students studying at the Bhiksu University of Sri Lanka in Anuradhapura and upgrading its circuit bungalow;

*	Construction of Staff quarters in the Trincomalee Campus of the  Eastern University.

- Ekala area, located closer to the Katunayake, Bandaranaike International Airport, provides a major contribution to the productions of the Industrial Sector in Sri Lanka. It has been designed to implement the following  sub‑projects under the Aero City Development project which is implemented under the Western Region Megapolis Plan. 

*	Establishment of  a reserved area for Value Added industries;

*	Establishment of  a reserved area for Small and Medium Scale Enterprises (SME);

*	Establishment of  a reserved area for commercial and recreational  activities;

*	Media City; and

*	Middle class housing and common amenities. 

The Aero City project inclusive of the above sub‑projects is due to be implemented under the Private Public Partnership basis. Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Patali Champika Ranawaka, the Minister of Megapolis and Western Development, to vest the land in extent of 80 acres belonging to the Sri Lanka Broadcasting Corporation to the Urban Development Authority, was approved by the Cabinet.

- The Chronic Kidney Disease of unknown etiology is currently prevalent in 60 Divisional Secretariat Divisions of 11 districts in Sri Lanka and has the possibility of being spread to other areas as well. It has become a serious community health issue and has created a number of social and economic problems as well. Accordingly, the proposal made by H.E. the President Maithripala Sirisena, to implement the following three projects with the collaboration of the Presidential Task Force on Prevention of Chronic Kidney Disease, the Ministry of Health, Nutrition and Indigenous Medicine and other stakeholder agencies  together with the labour contribution of the Sri Lanka Army and the Sri Lanka Navy, with a view to providing a healthcare service with enhanced medical, clinical and patient care facilities for the benefit of  the people affected by the Chronic Kidney Disease (with long term haemodialysis, long term peritoneal dialysis and kidney transplant) at an estimated cost of Rs.462.5 million, was approved by the Cabinet. 

*	Project for the construction of the Kidney Disease Patient Relief Care and Dialysis Centre of Kandy; 

*	Project for the construction of 'Methsirisevana' Kidney Disease Patient Relief Care and Dialysis Centre of Anuradhapura; 

*	Project for the construction of the Centre for Education and Information on Social Welfare and Economic Empowerment related to Kidney Disease in Girandurukotte.

- The government has focused its attention on the improvement of the Homagama - Pitipana road infrastructure facilities associated with the Tech City Development Plan implemented in accordance with the Western Region Megapolis Master Plan. Under this programme, the proposal made by Hon. Patali Champika Ranawaka, the Minister of Megapolis and Western Development, with a view to upgrading the road infrastructure facilities to ensure better connectivity of the transport network, to construct two new roads and to improve three existing roads with a cost estimate of Rs. 3,143 million as given below, was approved by the Cabinet.   

*	construction of a new access road with four lanes of 6.18km in length from Kottawa to Mahenwatta; 

*	construction of internal roads of 1.7km in length at the State Universities situated within the Mahenwatta cluster; 

*	improvement of 8.83km in the Pitipana Thalagala Road inclusive of the Dampe - Pitipana Road; 

*	improvement of the Uduwana Pansala Handiya Road with 2.9km in length; and

* 	improvement of access road from Kahathuduwa to Kottawa and up to the Thalagala Junction with 3km in length.

