- The proposal made by Hon. (Dr.) Wijayadasa Rajapaksa, the Minister of Justice, to sign a MOU between the Government Analyst's Department of Sri Lanka and the National Forensic Service of the Republic of Korea to extend cooperation on matters as follows, was approved by the Cabinet: 

*	Promotion of the standards of the Forensic testing of the Government Analyst's Department in order to achieve excellence in the process of investigations into criminal matters in Sri Lanka; 

*	Installation of new methodologies for the advancement of laboratory testing in the field of forensic science including new researches,   examination of questioned documents, forensic toxicology, analysis of narcotics, firearms and explosives  and the most modern analysis of DNA; 

*	Sharing the knowledge on testing methods pertaining to criminal investigations, the knowledge of the experts in the field, theoretical and practical knowledge in the conduct of criminal investigations; 

*	Exchange of knowledge mutually through workshops, seminars, training programmes and meetings conducted by experts; 

*	Granting assistance in the purchase of state-of-the-art laboratory equipment and tools; and  

*	Ensuring the international level of "Accredited Testing Samples" and the standards of the laboratories in the Government Analyst's Department.

- An Officials' Committee has been appointed, by the Cabinet to investigate into the activities of the Defence Headquarters Complex which is being constructed at Akuregoda, Battaramulla, and to report on same.  The attention of the Cabinet was drawn to the following facts mentioned in the Officials' Committee Report, with regard to a company to which the consultancy services of this project have been awarded, that is,  

*	the company has been selected without calling competitive bids; 

*	the company has been registered under the Registrar of Companies and under the Sri Lanka Institute of Architects, only after it has been selected  for consultancy services; 

* 	the  authorities concerned have not entered into a formal Agreement, to obtain services of the said company; 

* 	it has been decided to pay the said company Rs.606.032 million in cash, for the consultancy services of the entire project; Although, the company has been paid Rs.509.926 million as at 2015-05-31, (83% of the total payment) it has only completed 30.02% of the entire work of the project as of now. 

	The Cabinet decided to investigate into the above facts mentioned in the Officials' Committee Report pertaining to the Consultancy Services Company and to take legal action, on the findings of the  said investigation, as appropriate.

- Hon. Ravi Karunayake, the Minister of Finance, brought to the notice of the Cabinet that it has been planned to prepare the Budget for the year 2016, with a view to achieving the following objectives: 

*	to limit the Budget deficit to 5.5% of Gross Domestic Production (GDP); 

*	to maintain the public investments around 6% - 8% of GDP; 

*	to encourage the private investments to reach around 22% - 24% of GDP; 

* 	to raise the total investment level of the economy up to 30% of GDP; and 

*	to achieve an economic growth rate beyond 8% during the  Medium Term Framework of 2016 – 2018; 

	The Government, while successfully achieving the challenge of settling the unsettled liabilities accumulated for a long period, and creating a better treasury management system, has decided to introduce a Zero Based Budgeting Method as a tool of budget preparation and implementation from 2016 onwards. Accordingly, the Cabinet noted that it has been planned to prepare the Budget 2016 allocating the resources based on the sectoral ceilings, and the arrangements have been made to issue the 'Budget Call 2016' incorporating the guidelines and instructions for preparation of this year's Budget and that action will be taken to conduct preliminary discussions with the Ministries regarding the preparation of the Budget 2016 from first week of August 2015 onwards, on the information submitted by Hon. Ravi Karunayake, the Minister of Finance.

- Hon. Ranjith Maddumabandara, the Minister of Internal Transport, submitted to the Cabinet, the following information regarding the expenditure incurred by the institutions under the Ministry of Internal Transport, during the last Presidential Election period: 

*	the National Transport Commission has incurred an expenditure of Rs.8.2 million, for a meeting held between the former President and bus owners/ drivers/ conductors; 

*	the buses of the Sri Lanka Transport Board (SLTB)  have been deployed to transport people for political rallies and only an advance of Rs.50 million has been paid in that regard. The relevant invoices have been forwarded to the General Secretary of the United People's Freedom Alliance (UPFA), for the sum of Rs.142.5 million due to SLTB in that regard, but no action has yet been taken to settle the said bills; 

*	the Shalika Ground of the SLTB has been used for political activities and as a result the playground has been damaged and a stretch of the wall nearly 50 feet in length has been demolished and therefore a considerable expenditure would be incurred for the relevant renovation activities. 

	The Cabinet, having taken into consideration the above facts, decided to take legal action against the relevant parties in order to collect the relevant dues and obtain compensation for the losses caused.

- Expeditious completion of the Central Expressway is of paramount importance to the economic development of the country.  As per the recommendations of the Cabinet Sub-Committee on Economic Affairs, it has been proposed to implement its constructions through phasing out the entire length of this Expressway into four Sections, as follows: 

* Section-I	:	Kadawatha to Meerigama - with the loan assistance provided by the China Exim Bank;
	    
*  Section-II	:	Meerigama to Kurunegala (including the Ambepussa Link) - with the loan assistance provided by the Asian Development Bank;

*  Section-III  :	Pothuhera to Kandy - with the loan assistance provided by the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA);  

*  Section-IV :	Kurunegala to Dambulla - through local contractors utilizing the funds collected by local bond issue.   

The proposal made by Hon. Prime Minister Ranil Wickremasinghe, to appoint necessary Procurement Committees, Negotiation Committees and Loan Negotiation Committees, by the General Treasury, on the recommendations of the Cabinet Sub-Committee on Economic Affairs, to expedite the commencement of the project as above, was approved by the Cabinet.

- The proposal made by Hon. Ranil Wickramasinghe, Minister of Policy Planning, Economic Affairs, Child, Youth and Cultural Affairs to sign a Memorandum of Understanding between the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka and the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports in the State of Qatar was approved by the Cabinet. This Memorandum of Understanding covers mainly  the following areas.  

*	Promoting the corporation between the two countries for exchange of members of the Youth Organizations, experts and specialists etc., involved in the activities pertaining to youth policies; 

*	Promoting the corporation in physical as well as  economic activities relating to  youth and the exchange of ideas and information relevant to youth development between the two countries; 

*	Promoting the corporation between the two countries by exchanging ideas with regard to youth problems on the results of the field research activities; 

*	Implementation of youth sports, cultural exchange programmes and youth exchange programmes extensively between the two countries; 

*	Promoting corporation regarding the exchange of information pertaining to employment of the unemployed youth community; and 

*	Promoting cooperation between the two countries as agreed upon in the field of youth development.

- The proposal made by Hon. Ravi Karunayake, Minister of Finance, to present the Bills prepared by the Legal Draftsman to provide for the amendment to the following Acts in order to give effect to the Budget Proposals for 2015, in Parliament for approval, was approved by the Cabinet: 

		*	Inland Revenue Act, No. 10 of 2006;
*	Value Added Tax Act, No. 14 of 2002; 
*	Nation Building Tax Act, No. 9 of 2009; 
*	Finance Act; 
		*	Economic Service Charge Act, No. 13 of 2006; 
		*	Telecommunication Levy Act, No. 21 of 2011;		  		and
		*	Betting and Gaming Levy Act, No. 40 of 1988.

- The objective of this programme is to develop a framework needed to extend  cooperation in the field of sport research, development and excellence between the two countries based on the mutual performance and aspirations. In this regard, a greater focus will be made on the following aspects: 
*	Exchange of teams of Boxing, Golf, Karate, Taekwondo, Table Tennis, Volley Ball,  Baseball, Hockey, Squash and Cricket;
*	Exchange of sports training programmes and officials; 
*	Exchange of equipment required for development of sports related programmes; 
	
	Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Navin Dissanayake, Minister of Tourism and Sports, to enter into the above Memorandum of Understanding, was approved by the Cabinet.

- The proposal made by Hon. John Amarathunga, Minister of Public Order, Disaster Management and Christian Affairs, to enter into the above Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) drafted with a view to  strengthening the mutual cooperation on Disaster Management between Sri Lanka and Pakistan and thereby to creating an effective disaster management mechanism was approved by the Cabinet. In accordance with the MoU the Governments of Sri Lanka and Pakistan will collaborate in the field of  Disaster Management with regard to following aspects: 

		*	Community Shelter in Cyclone Affected System; 

*	Volunteer Based Early Warning System for Community Disaster Risk Reduction at Community Level; 

*	Enactment of Legislation regulating Development activities in the flood plains and wet lands in landslide soil erosion prone areas; 

*	Guidelines for participatory flood management; 

*	Early flood and landslide warning system; and  

*	Effective disaster relief services.

- The proposal made by Hon. John Amaratunga, Minister of Public Order, Disaster Management and Christian Affairs, to grant dual citizenship under a new Administrative Procedure, was approved by the Cabinet.  Accordingly, individuals who satisfy at least one of the following qualifications are eligible to apply for dual citizenship: 

 		*	who possesses professional or educational qualifications;
*	who owns immovable property worth of Rs.2.5 million or above;
*	who has a fixed deposit of Rs.2.5 million or above for a period of three (03) years in any Bank approved by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL);
*	who has fixed deposit of US$25,000 or above in NRFC/RFC/SFIDA accounts for a period of three (03) years in a Commercial Bank approved by the CBSL;
*	who has invested US$25,000 or above for three (03) years in a Treasury Bond or a Security Investment Account; and
  		*	on attaining the age of 55 years.

	Further, the spouse or unmarried children under the age of 22 years of the applicant are also eligible.

Hon. Thalatha Atukorala, Minister of Foreign Employment informed the Cabinet that the Ministry of Foreign Employment has made arrangements to implement a Welfare Programme for the Migrant workers through Sri Lanka Bureau of Foreign Employment, under the 100 Days Programme of the Government, with the participation and cooperation of the Ministry of Women's Affairs and the State Ministry of Children's Affairs. The Welfare Programmes to be implemented are as follows: 

(i)	Welfare and safety measures to be implemented in Pre Departure Stage;
(ii)	Welfare and safety measures to be implemented in Service Stage;
(iii)	Programmes to be implemented in Return  and Re-integration stage; and
(iv)	Welfare measures to be implemented for the family dependents of migrant workers.

- The above programme which was incorporated in the 100 Days Programme of the Government has been planned to be implemented as the “Healthy Motherhood Programme” effective from 21st March 2015 as proposed in the Budget Speech 2015. Accordingly - 

*	all pregnant mothers, registered and regularly attending the Government Antenatal Clinics are eligible for the proposed allowance on the recommendation of the Doctors;
*	nutritional food will be provided under this programme in installments of Rs.2,000/= each for a period of ten months including the last six months of pregnancy and the first four months after the child birth;
 *	the programme will be implemented by the Divisional Secretaries with the assistance of the Medical Officers and the Family Health Officers serving in the antenatal clinics under the supervision of the respective District Secretary/Government Agent on the direction  of the State Ministry for Child Affairs.

	The above proposal made by Hon. Ranil Wickremasinghe, the Prime Minister and the Minister of Policy Planning, Economic Affairs, Child, Youth and Cultural Affairs, was approved by the Cabinet.

