- the proposal made by H.E. the President, in his capacity as the Minister of Finance and Planning, to hold a Tax Week annually in order to create awareness among the public in respect of the Government Tax System and to declare the Tax Week from 01st to 08th November 2012, was approved by the Cabinet.  Its main objectives are as follows:

*	increase the awareness of the tax system among the public;

*	building public confidence in tax administration and related activities of the Departments of Inland Revenue and Sri Lanka Customs, whereby voluntary compliance with the taxes by the public could be encouraged;

*	enlighten the public of the contribution they could render towards future prosperity of the country through timely payment of taxes;

*	make the public aware of the tax reliefs and tax holidays granted by the new tax system which is being implemented since 2011;

- Cabinet considered  the recommendations of the Officials' Committee appointed for the purpose and approval was granted to implement the following recommendations of the Committee:

*	identification and mapping of fire prone areas in each District and preparation of Fire Management Plans for such areas;

*	supply of required resources, through the respective District Secretaries, to the Forest Department and the Department of Wildlife Conservation to ensure effective fire control programmes;

*	to advise the law enforcement authorities (Police and Armed Forces) to be vigilant, specially during the dry season, in order  to apprehend those responsible for initiating forest fires; and

*	conduct training and awareness programmes on fire management for communities and relevant stakeholders in the areas,

- the National Human Resources and Employment Policy lays down key policy priorities for human resource development and it covers a broad spectrum of subject areas including macro-economic and sectoral policies, small and medium enterprises, labour market policies for specific groups and training, management and career guidance.  The following are the objectives of the proposed National Human Resources and Employment Policy:
 
*	promote the attainment of full, productive and freely chosen employment for all men and women in Sri Lanka;

*	develop a highly competent, globally competitive, multi-skilled and productive work force;

*	improve income and quality of life of the working population across different sectors and regions;

*	enhance opportunities for workers to qualify for and to use their skills in jobs for which they are best suited; and

	On the above proposal made by Hon. D.E.W. Gunasekara, Minister (Senior) for Human Resources, Cabinet approval was granted for the preparation of an Action Plan, for implementation.

- On the  proposals made	by Hon. Bandula Gunawardena, Minister of Education for the	implementation of	the above programme, with the view to providing better	educational facilities to students throughout the country in keeping with the	policy of the Government, Cabinet approval was granted for the following:

*	to commence implementation of the above programme as decided upon by the Ministries of Finance and Planning; Economic Development; and Education,  utilizing the sum of Rs.2500 million allocated for this programme in the 2012 budget; 
 

*	action be taken to provide electricity, water, sanitation facilities and attend to other renovation work, identified on a priority basis, in the selected schools, through School Development Societies;

*	to provide science, computer, language, mathematics laboratories in identified secondary schools;

*	to recruit sufficient number of teachers for subjects such as Mathematics, Science, English Language, Information Technology and Sports etc., for schools where vacancies occur and action to be taken to enhance training facilities for such teachers; and

*	to entrust the construction works to suitable contractors in the respective areas selected on the recommendations of the respective District Secretaries and for the Central Engineering Consultancy Bureau (CECB)  to provide the necessary consultancy services for these construction works.

- the proposal made by Hon. Maithripala Sirisena, Minister of Health, to adopt several measures to curb the spreading of Rabies in several parts of the country, some of which are as follows, was approved by the Cabinet:

*	control of dog population;
*	implementation of an islandwide campaign for 	vaccination of dogs;
*	use of quality Rabies vaccine;
*	establish laboratory facilities for Rabies 	detection throughout the country; 	and
*	establish Rabies treatment units in hospitals.

-  the Report of the LLRC which was handed over to H.E. the President in mid November 2011, was placed before Parliament in mid December 2011.  The LLRC had made 285 recommendations which include many sub-recommendations and they have been classified into four main groups as follows: 

*	Recommendations relating to National Policy;
*	Recommendations pertaining to the final phase of the 	conflict;
*	Recommendations  related  to  human  rights  	and National security concerns; and
*	Recommendations  related  to  resettlement  and 	development.

	The Cabinet of Ministers appointed a Task Force headed by the Secretary to the President to monitor the implementation of the recommendations of the LLRC and an Action Plan prepared for implementation, submitted by H.E. the President, was approved by the Cabinet of Ministers.  Financial provision required for the implementation of the Action Plan will be provided by the General Treasury, depending on the requirement and within the stipulated time frame.  Funds for the purpose from 2013 onwards too will be provided by the General Treasury to the respective Institutions.

- provision of relief to the people living in the districts of Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa in the North Central Province and also those in other districts affected by the recent drought, was discussed.  Some of the urgent measures to be taken in this regard are as follows:

*	dredging of major irrigation reservoirs so as to increase there water retention capacities;

*	attending to renovation work of canal systems of major irrigation works;

*	renovation of agricultural wells;

*	provision of water pumps to locations where necessary;

*	providing seed paddy free of charge to affected farmers, for the next cultivation season; and

*	engaging members of affected families between the ages of 18 	and 60, in renovation work in the respective areas, making 	cash payments for the work done.

	It was also decided to appoint an Officials' Committee consisting of the Secretaries of the following Ministries, to oversee the expeditious implementation of the above:

	*	Ministry of Irrigation and Water Resources Management;
	*	Ministry of Economic Development;
	*	Ministry of Agriculture;
	*	Ministry of Agrarian Services and Wildlife; and
	*	Ministry of Disaster Management.

- the objective of setting up of this centre in Sri Lanka will be to contribute to improving the quality of education in Sri Lanka and in the South Asian Region, through high quality teacher development. It is expected to enroll 200 local participants and 200 foreign participants each year for long term professional courses. This centre is to provide full time residential courses as follows:

		*	Master in Teacher Education (one year/full time);
		*	Postgraduate Diploma in Teacher Education (one year/full time); 
		*	Short Term Refresher Courses for Continuing Teacher Education 			(three months each/at least two courses per year); and,
		*	Thematic Courses (5 - 12 days each/at least four courses per year).    

	the above proposal made by Hon. Bandula Gunawardena, Minister of Education was approved by the Cabinet. The total cost of the project amounting to Rs.587 million will be met with financial provision to be provided under the Medium Term Budgetary Provision 2013-2016.

- the proposal made by Hon. Dinesh Gunawardena, Minister of Water Supply and Drainage, for the National Water Supply and Drainage Board to carry out the following urgent improvements needed to upgrade the above two (02) water supply schemes, to meet the prevailing demand for drinking water in the area, was approved by the Cabinet:

*	improvements to Elletota intake;

*	supply and installation of two (02) low lift pumps and two (02) booster pumps at Aluthwela;

*	improvements to Aluthwela intake;

*	improvements to Diyatalawa water treatment plant;

*	improvements to Bandarawela water treatment plant; and

*	intake dam improvement at Kahagolla and Rohampton.

		The increased output from both treatment plants can provide adequate drinking water to 7000 additional service connections.  The total estimated cost of the project is Rs.252.7 million and the National Water Supply and Drainage Board will secure the funds necessary as a loan from a State Bank.

- a Ministerial Committee consisting of Hon. Piyasena Gamage, Minister (Senior) for National Resources and Hon. Felix Perera, Minister of Social Services, was appointed by the Cabinet recently to go into this matter and make recommendations.  Public Assistance Monthly Allowance has been increased from Rs.500/- to Rs.1000/- in keeping with the Budget Proposals 2012  and beneficiaries over 70 years of age are entitled to receive Rs.1000/- per month. The following recommendations made by the above Ministerial Committee will be considered for implementation by the relevant authorities:

*	expansion of nursing facilities at household level to provide health care services for elders to be implemented by the Ministry of Health;

*	rehabilitation and reconstruction of elders' wards at Base Hospitals of the Ministry of Health and major hospitals belonging to the Ministry of Indigenous Medicine;

*	encouraging the private sector to construct Elders' Homes;

*	construction of new Elders Homes in each Province so as to accommodate destitute elders in the respective Provincial Council areas;

*	initiate a sponsor scheme to enable Sri Lankans living abroad to make contributions for the maintenance of Elders Homes; and

*	extension of the Agrahara Insurance Scheme to include parents above 60 years of age, of employees in the Public Sector.

- the objective of this proposal is to achieve self-sufficiency in meat and eggs in 2016 by promoting small , medium and large scale poultry farmers.  The proposal made by Hon. Arumugan Thondaman, Minister of  Livestock and Rural Community Development,  to provide the following facilities to achieve this objective, was approved by the Cabinet:

*	introduce self-employment opportunities and establish 4,500 new small scale poultry farms;

*	establish 75 Mini Hatcheries;

*	establish 100 Mini Processing Units;

*	expand cold storage facilities;

*	introduce 100 Mobile Sales Units ; and,

*	provide training facilities on poultry farming and disease control, to poultry farmers and officers.

It was decided that this programme should be implemented in collaboration with the 'Divineguma' Programme launched by the Ministry of Economic Development.

- the expected paddy harvest from Yala Season 2011 and Maha Season 2011/2012 is around 4.5 million MT.  With the expected paddy harvest in Maha Season 2012, there will be a stock of 3.2 million MT of paddy with the Paddy Marketing Board.   According to estimates, the annual consumption of rice in the country is around 3.54 million MT.  and with the collection of paddy of the Maha and Yala Seasons 2012, there is to be a surplus of 1.01 million MT of paddy.  The proposals made by H.E. the President in this regard, in his capacity as the Minister of Finance and Planning, some of which are as follows, were approved by the Cabinet:

*	provision of Rs.1280 million from State Banks to the Paddy Marketing board for the purchase of paddy;

*	provision of Rs.1015 million, made up as follows, to the District Secretaries for the purchase of paddy - 

	Pollonnaruwa Rs.250 million, Matale Rs.50 million, Kurunegala Rs.100 million, Moneragala Rs.30 million, Kilinochchi Rs.70 million, Mannar Rs.30 million, Hambantota Rs.10 million, Batticaloa Rs.25 million, Trincomalee Rs.50 million, Anuradhapura Rs.200 million, Ampara Rs.200 million;

*	provision of Rs.15,000 million by the Bank of Ceylon, Rs.5000 million by the Peoples' Bank and Rs.1500 million by the Regional Development Bank, to provide loans to the private sector for the purchase of paddy;

*	provision of Rs.2000 million by State Banks to Co-operative Societies for the purchase of paddy;

*	to call for worldwide tenders to export the surplus of paddy;

*	to utilize the stores complex constructed at Oyamaduwa under "Deyata Kirula", for the storage of paddy; and,

*	to provide a stock of 500 MT of paddy to the World Food Organization (WFO), to which they have already consented.

- Twenty (T/20) World Cricket Championship is scheduled to be held around September/October 2012 in Sri Lanka and 12 Member countries are expected to take part.  There will be 20 teams comprising both males and females and altogether 52 matches will be played in the tournament.  The following grounds are to be made available for the purpose: 

*	R. Premadasa International Cricket Ground ;
*	Mahinda Rajapaksa International Cricket Ground ;
*	Pallekele International Cricket Ground ; and
*	Rangiri Dambullu International Cricket Ground.

		It is proposed to hold the tournament for 20 days and the ICC has agreed to provide a direct grant of 9.4 million US Dollars to Sri Lanka as expenses of holding the tournament and has also planned to spend a further 30 million US Dollars in Sri Lanka as expenses in the tournament. The proposal made by Hon. Mahindananda Aluthgamage, Minister of Sports, to proceed with the programme of work, was approved by the Cabinet.

